# publicise deny-list — strings that must never reach the published copy.
#
# One fixed string per line. Matched against the OUTPUT of tests/publicise.sh, after
# every redaction rule has run, in any capitals and any Unicode spelling of the same text
# ("STRASSE" for "straße", a decomposed "é" for a precomposed one). A look-alike letter
# from another script does not match. A hit refuses the transform and writes nothing, so
# the file this repo publishes cannot contain it. The list must be valid UTF-8, one token
# per line (a line ends at LF only), and a character newer than your python3's Unicode
# tables refuses the transform rather than going unmatched.
#
# This is the EXAMPLE. The real list is machine-local and is deliberately not committed
# here: a deny-list in a public repo publishes the exact strings it exists to withhold.
#
#   cp tests/publicise-deny.example ~/.claude/publicise-deny.txt
#   $EDITOR ~/.claude/publicise-deny.txt
#
# Override the path with PUBLICISE_DENY=/some/other/file.
#
# Good entries are the names that only mean something inside your own setup: private
# project and product lines, private repo names, your username, absolute home paths,
# and the filenames of any local file a redaction rule folds out.

YOUR-PRIVATE-PROJECT
your-private-source-repo
your-username
/Users/
/home/

# Machine-state claims are the other class worth a row: the names of hooks, plugins and
# scripts wired into YOUR harness ("the X hook goes through this wrapper" is a claim
# about the reader's machine), and the private incidents you cite as precedent ("the
# 2026-01-01 audit", "the ledger that was N× low"). A redaction rule handles the ones
# already known; a row here catches the next one before it ships.
your-hook-name
your-private-incident
